A||||16||Brian Olsovsky||Firebird injectors for more fuel||||1||
Z||000000||Brian Olsovsky||12-12-1999||11:33 PM||||I have built a 3.1 stroker out of my 85 gt 6cyl mpi. It has freeflow exhaust, 3 angle valvejob and milled heads, turned crank with a .060 overbore on the pistons, Jacobs ignition Hypertech EVERYTHING, ported exhaust headers, auto tranny with a shift kit from the Fiero Store, 16" custom wheels and tires and a Holley side scoop with K/N filter. HERE'S THE PROBLEM!!! this car is a real killer on takeoff, but after second or third gear i'm either running out of gas, or my cam isn't letting me go any faster. maybe larger injectors or a pressure regulator would help me keep going. i've been told that firebird injectors are a money saving trick if you can find some. i've been told that Fiero injectors flow somewhere around 13.3 whereas the firebird injectors are about 18.0. please correct if i'm mistaken. would this help my top end starvation problem??? am i needing air or fuel??? or am i simply running out of gears???||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-13-1999||12:12 AM||||I don't know anything about motors, but I wouldn't mind seeing the car. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||superXmotorsports||12-13-1999||12:26 AM||||I would start with the Hypertech adjustable fuel pressure regulator and see what a difference it makes... SXM.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Shaun41178||12-13-1999||04:04 AM||||Brian:  Sounds like a nice engine you have built up there.  <P>The Fiero injectors are rated at 15 pounds per hour while the Firebirds came in two flow rates.  19 and 22 lb/hr.  I would get an air/fuel gauge to see if you are running lean on the top end.  I doubt that you are running lean as the 3.1 engines from the factory used the same injectors as the Fiero.<P>If you are going to go with a adjustable fuel pressure regulator then I still recommend on getting a air/fuel gauge.  Also if you are looking to fine tune your car and to try and figure out if you are running lean or if it is just the gears, then I would try to get to a dyno and have it dyno tested.  They have a lot of equipment and measureing devices to tell you what your engine is doing and could probably tell you if you are running lean or if it is just the tranny.<P>My personal opinion is that you are running out of gears.  I have a 3 spd auto as well and once I hit third my car becomes a dag.  I try to stay away from races in the top end cause I will almost lose everytime.<P>Tell me about the shift kit.  i purchased the same one from the Fiero Store but have yet to install it.  I have been told that it changes the shift points at WOT.  Is this true?  I like where my tranny shifts now which is right at 5500 rpm at WOT and I don't want that to change.  Fromthe shift kit I just want the shifts to be faster but at the same rpm.  Any info about the shift kit would be great.  You can email me at Shaun41178@aol.com if you prefer.  thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||12-13-1999||06:43 AM||||FOCOA did a four part article on stroking the 2.8L very similar to your rebuild.  They do mention that you will need more fuel than the stock injectors will deliver with the stock fuel pressure regulator.<P>The fix is to either install Larger injectors (such as from a small block chevy v-8) or install an adjustable pressure regulator and crank up the pressure on your stock injectors.<P>Ed Parks has told me tha same thing about upgrading the the 3.4L engine.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||12-13-1999||09:30 AM||||Shaun--I've read here on the forum that you need something like 45# of fuel, so I don't think that the injectors would work.  (Am I confused?  Am I talking about something totally different?)  And I would say NOT to get a shift kit.  My mechanic said that it wouldn't help any.  And, more importantly (IMHO), theogre said no.  He said to change the filter and fluid and it would make a big difference.  Brian, if you don't know anything about theogre, search for his posts.  He knows alot about this stuff (as well as others-I don't want to leave anybody out!).||||reg||1
Z||000006||superXmotorsports||12-13-1999||10:24 AM||||Yes monkeyman that's 45 PSI (fuel pressure) with the adjustable fuel pressure regulator.<BR>SXM.||||reg||3
Z||000007||Eric||12-13-1999||04:20 PM||||Is the fuel pressure regulator the key to making big horsepower?  I've just been motoring around in my brand new 3.1 and I noticed a ton more torque (I jumped on it in forth and was at 80mph before I knew it), but I don't want to see how it behaves above 4,000 rpm yet, seeing as how the engine is brand new.  I also have the GM hipo cam and I think it's more of a high rpm unit than the stocker.  I bet a little more fuel in the upper portion of the rpm band would help out greatly.  Guess I better call Summit to see why they haven't sent my Accel regulator yet or maybe I just get one through the Fiero Store.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Shaun41178||12-13-1999||05:58 PM||||According to the Chiltons manual all the fuel pressure regulators from the factory were between 32-47 psi at the regulator.  Before you go out and spend $65 on an adjustable regulator get a fuel pressure guage and see what the pressure is set at.  Some of you might already be at 45 psi or even at 40 which would be enough.<P>I run nitrous and I don't run lean on the top end of the rpm range so what makes some of you people think that a 3.2 will need more fuel pressure then a stock 2.8?  Sure it might but it will only be about 3-4 psi difference.  According to the Chiltons the 85 GT had 41-47  psi at the regulator.  so what would be accomplished by spending $65 on a regulator to adjust it to 45 psi?  <P>If you go with larger injectors then you will need a custom burned chip to get maximum performance and fuel economy.<P>Like I said before the 3.1 and 2.8's from the factory used the same fuel pressure regulators and fuel injectors.<P>But if you people want to go out and spend the money on a new fuel pressure reg then go for it.  But in order to make sure that the new regulator is making a difference you will have to get to a dyno to have the air/fuel ratio measured or get an air/fuel guage.  This will have to be done cause you won't be able to "feel" the difference if the engine is running lean or not.  I personally don't feel that one is needed unless you are going for the maximum power of the engine and have the time and money to fine tune your fuel system so you have a 12.5:1 air/fuel ratio to get maximum power.  If you are not willing to go this route then I see it as pointless to get one cause by giving the engien too much fuel can hurt performance instead of help it.  Choice is yours I guess.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||12-13-1999||06:50 PM||||I'm just going by what Ed Parks of the Fiero Factory and Fiero Doc of FOCOA have said about needing more fuel for a performance 3.1 and beyond.  I have no experience on this to draw on.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Brian Olsovsky||12-13-1999||07:29 PM||||these are good replies but ive found some firebird injectors for about $150 used. if i simply install these injectors, will i gain performance?? i don't want to start throwing parts at a problem, i want to solve it without buying things i don't need.||||reg||5
Z||000011||Cooter||12-13-1999||07:44 PM||||Get the Air/fuel meter first. I have one in my DuckMobile because I was concerned about running lean at high speed/throttle positions. It was not very expensive and available from Summit-brand name was Intellitronix or something like that.||||reg||1
Z||000012||terryk||12-13-1999||08:11 PM||||Don't install them. Spend the money on an O2 meter and/or EGT. Then use those readings to make an educated decision if more or less fuel is needed. We've run the stock injectors on 3.1L iron heads and they work fine.<P>If you have access to a scanner (Diacom, Tech 1, Tech 2), look at the Integrator and Block Learn Multiplier while cruising, and look at the O2 voltage at WOT. Those will tell you what you need to do.<P>TK<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||mwbackus||12-13-1999||08:44 PM||||Not that I would recommend different injectors in this particular case, but I do have a suggestion for anyone who is considering $150 for "used" injectors.  Brand new Ford Motorsports M9593C302 injectors are available from Summit for around $200 (set of 8).  They are 19 lb injectors and have the same design as the multec injectors used in the GM MPFI systems (ie.. they fit perfectly and have the correct resistance).  Anyone doing a 3.4 swap could use these lower cost injectors, much better than paying over $300 for 6 new 3.4 injectors.<BR>Although the ECM can not compensate for a flow increase of 5 lbs on a stock 2.8, there are ways to modify the MAP sensor voltage which could enable the use of these higher flow injectors on the these engines.<P><BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||12-14-1999||12:25 AM||||Brian, in your first post you wondered if your cam was to blame. You mentioned a bunch of mods, but didn't say if you replaced the cam. Exactly what cam do you have in it. If it's a factory 3.1 cam, then they have a bunch of low end torque, but not much top end.||||reg||1
Z||000015||fastonpumpgas||12-14-1999||08:26 AM||||Batboy is right you need a higher grind cam if you want anything above 4000 .  I heard that you can put 3.1 injectors in and an ajustable reg in and that will keep up with a 70 shot.  I think you start at 56 lbs at idle and never exceeding 128 lbs.  Definately need a FPG , I think you can play with it alitlle to get a good pressure, but I would go with at least a 26o cam.  I got mind from Comp for an S-10 2.8 upgrade.  It was 125 bucks||||reg||1
Z||000016||fastonpumpgas||12-14-1999||08:29 AM||||Oh yeah I would lap and back angle your vavles if you ever have the heads off, this was a week point for fieros.  maybe you arent getting the exhaust out quick enough...  just a thought||||reg||1
